    perf(lane-target): resolve the git binary once instead of PATH-probing it on every spawn (PERF-016)
    
    Both scripts/lane_target.py and tests/test_lane_target.py launched git as a bare `git`
    in argv, so CPython's execvp walked PATH and issued one failing execve per entry before
    reaching the real binary. Resolve it once at import time and reuse it for every call.
    
    AGENTS.md rule 2 (every check must be able to fail) is preserved: no check, assertion,
    exit code or row was added, removed or re-worded. The gate still emits 193 PASS rows and
    19 genuine fixture FAIL rows, with PASS row text identical apart from the board-lock
    adjudicator's dynamic `holder=pid-####` field.
    
    Measured at 2bb114fd in one window (loadavg 6.84-8.50), paired base/patch clones with the
    arms alternated back-to-back, three groups each, CPU = user+sys:
    
      tests/test_lane_target.py   1.747 s -> 1.683 s   (ranges overlap: 1.53-1.88 vs 1.56-1.85)
      bash tests/selftest.sh     16.89 s -> 16.22 s   (ranges overlap: 15.74-18.48 vs 15.83-16.71)
    
    No CPU win is claimed: both deltas sit inside this gate's measured ~1.4 s instrument floor.
    The resolvable result is the syscall count:
    
      execve attempts 2401 -> 168, of which ENOENT PATH probes 2294 -> 61
      python3 -B tests/test_lane_target.py: Ran 40 tests, OK in both arms
    
    Measurement record, commands and both directions of the claim:
    docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-04-PERF-016.md
    
